WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:05.166
*Siren blows*

00:00:05.666 --> 00:00:06.950
This is not a drill.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:08.466
Get to a safe distance.

00:00:08.466 --> 00:00:10.550
This is the biggest bomb
that we have ever made.

00:00:10.933 --> 00:00:11.833
Do not look.

00:00:11.966 --> 00:00:12.900
I repeat,

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:13.716
do not

00:00:13.716 --> 00:00:15.416
look at the bomb.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.250
Now I am become film theorist,

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.416
destroyer of childhoods.

00:00:31.516 --> 00:00:32.633
Hello Internet!

00:00:32.633 --> 00:00:37.400
Welcome to Film Theory, the show
that sometimes actually talks about films.

00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:39.750
Hey, look, it's not my fault
that most of the interesting stuff

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.183
these days is happening on the Internet.

00:00:41.183 --> 00:00:44.300
But shockingly enough,
between all the shorts and toilet wars,

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.150
Hollywood finally managed to produce
a movie that caught all of our attention.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:49.100
Oppenheimer.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.766
And honestly, with Hollywood strikes
nowhere close to ending, this is probably

00:00:52.766 --> 00:00:56.183
one of the last times will actually get
to talk about a new movie for a while.

00:00:56.183 --> 00:00:57.650
So let's just do that, shall we?

00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:59.483
Let's theorize
for a bit about Oppenheimer,

00:00:59.483 --> 00:01:03.150
because spoiler alert, it's
not actually about the title character.

00:01:03.166 --> 00:01:05.266
Now, for those of you
who aren't looking to pay 20 bucks

00:01:05.266 --> 00:01:08.483
to go see 3 hours of men
dramatically talking at each other,

00:01:08.483 --> 00:01:11.100
this is the latest movie from director
Christopher Nolan.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.533
And it's all about the life of J.

00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:15.983
Robert Oppenheimer, a brilliant scientist
who was recruited by the U.S.

00:01:15.983 --> 00:01:18.683
government in World War II
to develop the atomic bomb.

00:01:18.716 --> 00:01:21.166
While a great deal of the film covers
the development of the bomb.

00:01:21.166 --> 00:01:25.050
Most of the drama actually comes into play
with the fallout of the bomb.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:26.266
Pun definitely intended.

00:01:26.266 --> 00:01:30.050
You see, Oppenheimer felt intense guilt
for the creation of his weapon, fearing

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.716
that the escalation of nuclear weaponry
would lead to worldwide annihilation.

00:01:33.716 --> 00:01:36.083
And so he used his influence
to try to get the United States

00:01:36.083 --> 00:01:40.016
to back off from the arms race
that he himself inadvertently created.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:42.316
Of course, this doesn't sit too
well with the powers that be,

00:01:42.316 --> 00:01:45.650
and they ultimately try to discredit him
for their own political gain.

00:01:45.683 --> 00:01:49.133
It is heavy stuff with a lot of super
serious themes on its mind.

00:01:49.166 --> 00:01:49.766
Personally, though,

00:01:49.766 --> 00:01:53.716
my favorite part of the whole thing was
when Oppenheimer said “It's Oppin’ time.”

00:01:53.716 --> 00:01:55.433
Then he Opped all over the screen.

00:01:55.433 --> 00:01:58.150
It’s completely unexpected,
very good twist in the third act.

00:01:58.166 --> 00:02:01.133
But like I alluded to in the intro,
I think there's more going on here.

00:02:01.166 --> 00:02:01.650
Oh, sure.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.766
It's an incredible commentary
on the effects of power,

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:04.883
whether or not we should be toying

00:02:04.883 --> 00:02:08.216
with destructive atomic energy
and how ambition can cloud your judgment.

00:02:08.216 --> 00:02:12.650
But underneath the surface, there's a
second story going on here, a meta story.

00:02:12.683 --> 00:02:13.800
I believe that Oppenheimer

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.966
is not just an overt biopic
about the father of the atomic bomb.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.066
I also think it's a secret biopic
about the man

00:02:20.066 --> 00:02:23.050
who made it, the director
Christopher Nolan himself.

00:02:23.066 --> 00:02:25.283
And what's more,
if you look at the direction Oppenheimer's

00:02:25.283 --> 00:02:29.816
life took in the film, it tells us exactly
what's next for Nolan in his career.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:32.566
Here's a hint for you. *lightsaber
sound* Oh, yeah.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:34.016
So strap on your goggles

00:02:34.016 --> 00:02:38.066
and don't look directly at the explosion
friends, it's time to set this theory off.

00:02:38.116 --> 00:02:40.500
So I imagine
your first thought here is probably “ha?”

00:02:40.516 --> 00:02:43.583
Suggesting that Nolan’s making a movie
this meta doesn't really seem

00:02:43.583 --> 00:02:46.466
like the sort of thing
that the super serious director would do.

00:02:46.616 --> 00:02:48.783
Does it? Well, you would think so.
But here's the thing.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:49.700
He's done it before.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.216
Nolan's first big budget movie
after he hit the mainstream

00:02:52.216 --> 00:02:54.916
with Batman Begins
and The Dark Knight was Inception.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:57.916
Now if you don't know Inception,
you most definite know its influence.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:00.000
Memes about this thing were everywhere.

00:03:00.016 --> 00:03:01.166
It is the sole reason

00:03:01.166 --> 00:03:04.483
that every trailer from the 20 teens
had those BWAAHs in them.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:05.116
You know the ones. To

00:03:13.016 --> 00:03:13.883
give you a quick summary.

00:03:13.883 --> 00:03:16.216
Inception was a classic heist film
with a twist.

00:03:16.216 --> 00:03:18.400
The heists were happening
inside of dreams.

00:03:18.416 --> 00:03:21.300
It's a really cool film,
and it truly pioneered a visual style

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.316
that would influence movies like Doctor
Strange in the years that would follow.

00:03:24.316 --> 00:03:24.916
But it was also

00:03:24.916 --> 00:03:28.983
one of the first and biggest movies
to truly lean into theory culture.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.216
For instance,
the movie ends on this big cliffhanger

00:03:31.216 --> 00:03:34.133
that, if you pay enough attention
to the small details like a true theorist

00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:36.516
throughout the rest of the movie,
you can actually solve.

00:03:36.533 --> 00:03:37.550
And that wasn’t it.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.916
Years and years ago,
there was this popular theory,

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:43.066
A FAN THEORY going around
suggesting that the entire movie

00:03:43.066 --> 00:03:44.816
was actually one big allegory

00:03:44.816 --> 00:03:47.033
for the art of filmmaking;
with each of the main characters

00:03:47.033 --> 00:03:49.016
representing a different member
of the film's crew.

00:03:49.016 --> 00:03:50.633
You had the extractor organizing

00:03:50.633 --> 00:03:52.516
the whole thing,
representing a film's director,

00:03:52.516 --> 00:03:56.033
the point man who did all the research
representing producers, the architect

00:03:56.033 --> 00:03:57.016
who created the dreams

00:03:57.016 --> 00:04:00.916
themselves, representing the production
designer, the forger to impersonate others

00:04:00.916 --> 00:04:04.700
in the dream, representing actors,
a benefactor, financing the whole project

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:08.300
and meddling a little bit more than they
probably should representing the studios.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:11.666
Even the mark that they were implanting
the ideas in to represent

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.716
the audience of the movie
being affected by the film's ideas.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:16.316
It was a really cool, really

00:04:16.316 --> 00:04:20.666
classic film theory that, believe or not,
Nolan himself actually confirmed.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.366
He explained that Inception
was a reflection of his artistic life

00:04:23.366 --> 00:04:25.483
and that he wrote the team
based collaborative activity

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:26.866
from the movie, based on the team

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:29.516
based collaborative process
that he knew: Filmmaking.

00:04:29.516 --> 00:04:31.366
Which just goes to show us
two separate things.

00:04:31.366 --> 00:04:35.000
First, sometimes those super out, there
theories aren't as wrong as you think.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:35.633
And two,

00:04:35.633 --> 00:04:39.833
Nolan isn't afraid to use allegories
about making movies in his movies.

00:04:39.833 --> 00:04:42.950
So now that we've established that fact,
how can we map Nolan's life

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:43.950
onto Oppenheimer?

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:46.733
Well, Oppenheimer is praised
as a thought leader in his field.

00:04:46.733 --> 00:04:49.516
He's brilliant, a true visionary
who was able to get the job done.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:52.666
All of that maps pretty perfectly
onto Nolan, who is undeniably

00:04:52.666 --> 00:04:55.733
one of the best, most respected directors
currently working today.

00:04:55.733 --> 00:04:57.533
Just like Oppy, Nolan is a great leader

00:04:57.533 --> 00:05:00.083
with other professionals
who are very eager to work with him.

00:05:00.083 --> 00:05:02.183
I mean, just look at the star power
in Oppenheimer

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:04.900
that are basically regulated
to cameos and background extras.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.650
A great specific example of this.

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:08.366
A few years ago,
Matt Damon was taking a break

00:05:08.366 --> 00:05:10.016
from acting to spend time with his family.

00:05:10.016 --> 00:05:12.083
He negotiated with his wife
that the only thing

00:05:12.083 --> 00:05:15.450
that would get him to end his time off
early was if Christopher Nolan called.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:18.266
Right before Nolan offered him
his part in Oppenheimer.

00:05:18.266 --> 00:05:20.333
But Nolan's got this respect
from being a thought

00:05:20.333 --> 00:05:22.283
leader who's unapologetic for his work.

00:05:22.283 --> 00:05:25.316
In a cinema ecosystem,
where only established IP gets substantial

00:05:25.316 --> 00:05:28.816
budgets, Nolan is able to get
almost any project that he wants funded.

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:30.166
Again, just like Oppenheimer.

00:05:30.166 --> 00:05:31.800
Who at the beginning of his career
was considered

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.350
to be out there with his theories,

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:36.716
but by the end of it was getting huge
funding for his Manhattan Project.

00:05:36.716 --> 00:05:40.250
And you cannot deny that
both of these men get explosive results.

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:44.450
Since Batman Begins, Nolan's films
almost universally get tons of prestige

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:44.933
and money.

00:05:44.933 --> 00:05:45.833
As for Oppenheimer,

00:05:45.833 --> 00:05:49.133
his explosive results are measured
in, you know, actual explosions.

00:05:49.133 --> 00:05:51.166
But for all the good
that Nolan and Oppenheimer share,

00:05:51.166 --> 00:05:53.450
you could argue that they also share
a lot of the bad.

00:05:53.483 --> 00:05:57.083
For instance, ego and self-importance
were Oppenheimer's biggest flaws.

00:05:57.083 --> 00:05:59.850
It had to be his way
or the highway. Mapping that onto Nolan.

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:00.016
Well,

00:06:00.016 --> 00:06:03.616
one could look at the disastrous decision
to release the movie Tenet in theaters

00:06:03.616 --> 00:06:06.866
in September of 2020,
at the height of the COVID 19 pandemic.

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:07.616
As one article

00:06:07.616 --> 00:06:11.383
so succinctly put it, quote,
I don't want to die for Chris Nolan's ego.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:14.233
Sounds a lot like something someone
might say about Oppenheimer, doesn't it?

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:17.683
Another example of Nolan's brilliant
stubbornness, are all the criticisms

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:21.983
around his sound mixing on multiple movies
due to, quote, artistic choices.

00:06:21.983 --> 00:06:25.216
All of Nolan's most recent films
have been criticized for having dialog

00:06:25.216 --> 00:06:28.316
that's borderline impossible
to hear, as Forbes put it, quote,

00:06:28.316 --> 00:06:30.616
I don't know what Chris Nolan has against
dialog.

00:06:30.616 --> 00:06:33.016
There were widespread reports of audiences
having trouble

00:06:33.016 --> 00:06:34.850
hearing several characters
speak in The Dark Knight

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:38.133
Rises, Interstellar,
Dunkirk, Tenet and Oppenheimer.

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:41.183
Nolan has gone on record saying that
he knows that his movies will be watched

00:06:41.183 --> 00:06:43.133
across all sorts of different screens.

00:06:43.133 --> 00:06:46.016
In good cinemas
and bad theaters, televisions, iPhones.

00:06:46.016 --> 00:06:48.533
But as one sound professional
who worked with him put it, quote,

00:06:48.533 --> 00:06:50.400
I know Nolan does like to push
the envelope.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:51.016
He's an artist,

00:06:51.016 --> 00:06:54.350
and I don't think he believes in working
to the lowest common denominator.

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:57.150
Sounds like a person
whose ego doesn't allow him to compromise.

00:06:57.166 --> 00:07:00.316
Kind of like a certain scientist
pursuing results no matter the odds.

00:07:00.333 --> 00:07:01.616
So you can certainly see that

00:07:01.616 --> 00:07:03.833
there's at least some parallels
between these two men.

00:07:03.833 --> 00:07:05.333
But what about their stories?

00:07:05.333 --> 00:07:07.600
Well, follow me here,
but the dramatized version of Oppy's life

00:07:07.616 --> 00:07:10.700
from the movie tracks
really nicely with Nolan's career.

00:07:10.733 --> 00:07:13.166
Both have humble beginnings
and advanced through their respective

00:07:13.166 --> 00:07:15.150
fields,
through smarts, hard work and talent.

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:18.233
Both are hungry and aspire to greatness,
eventually catching the attention

00:07:18.233 --> 00:07:19.066
of bigger players.

00:07:19.066 --> 00:07:21.233
For Oppenheimer,
his brilliance makes him a candidate

00:07:21.233 --> 00:07:23.600
to lead a secret program
by the American government.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.566
For Nolan, his track record
makes him a serious contender to direct

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:29.116
bigger and bigger movies for mainstream
Hollywood studios,

00:07:29.116 --> 00:07:31.466
where he eventually gets swept up
by Warner Brothers.

00:07:31.466 --> 00:07:34.016
And both men
have themselves their pet ambitions.

00:07:34.016 --> 00:07:37.100
One of Oppenheimer's biggest goals
early in his career was to pioneer

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:39.000
the study of physics in the United States.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.350
He specifically turns down

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:42.766
offers to teach and research in Europe
because he wanted to build

00:07:42.766 --> 00:07:45.333
an American program
that would become a scientific leader.

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:48.683
Similarly, Nolan views himself
as a pioneer in the use of camera

00:07:48.683 --> 00:07:52.733
technology in film,
specifically the IMAX camera to get larger

00:07:52.733 --> 00:07:54.266
than life shots in his movies.

00:07:54.266 --> 00:07:56.450
Every single one of his movies
since The Dark Knight

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:58.800
has used this technology
and IMAX has since

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:01.800
become a staple of big budget
cinematic experiences.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:04.733
Which leads us then to the biggest part
of this whole thing, the bomb.

00:08:04.733 --> 00:08:05.516
For Oppenheimer.

00:08:05.516 --> 00:08:07.883
His bomb is easy.
It is literally the atomic bomb,

00:08:07.883 --> 00:08:09.716
which would change the course of his life
forever.

00:08:09.716 --> 00:08:13.166
For Nolan, though,
his atomic weapon is a bit more symbolic.

00:08:13.166 --> 00:08:16.700
His bomb is the superhero
mega franchise, specifically

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:20.033
Batman, which would also forever
change the course of his career.

00:08:20.033 --> 00:08:23.483
Here we have two leaders with
incredibly specific and different visions

00:08:23.483 --> 00:08:24.616
of how they go about things.

00:08:24.616 --> 00:08:27.883
Against protocol, Oppenheimer gathers
the most brilliant minds that he can find

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:31.066
regardless of their gender and past
affiliations, wrangling them all together

00:08:31.066 --> 00:08:34.633
in Los Alamos in the middle of nowhere,
and in a world increasingly moving towards

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:38.933
CGI and special effects action, Nolan's
Batman movies were really grounded.

00:08:38.933 --> 00:08:41.183
They were down to earth.
They were practically shot.

00:08:41.183 --> 00:08:43.283
And in the end, both men's methods worked.

00:08:43.283 --> 00:08:47.516
The Oppenheimer led Trinity test produces
the atomic bomb and the Nolan led movie

00:08:47.516 --> 00:08:48.750
production created the Dark

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:52.883
Knight trilogy, which became some of
the most successful movies of the 2000s.

00:08:52.916 --> 00:08:55.033
But what happens after this
incredible success?

00:08:55.033 --> 00:08:58.200
The people who funded those projects
turn around and use them in ways

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.283
that Nolan and Oppenheimer
don't approve of.

00:09:00.283 --> 00:09:03.450
Both the United States and other countries
like the Soviet Union kept

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:07.400
pushing the development of these atomic
explosives, much to Oppenheimer's horror.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:10.666
While Warner Brothers and other major
motion picture studios kept creating

00:09:10.666 --> 00:09:13.733
massive superhero films
using The Dark Knight as their template.

00:09:13.733 --> 00:09:14.600
One specific parallel

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:18.016
that I could draw here is President Truman
standing in for Warner Brothers.

00:09:18.016 --> 00:09:21.050
Without Oppenheimer's knowledge
or consent the bombs created at Los

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:22.816
Alamos were deployed in the field.

00:09:22.816 --> 00:09:27.050
Meanwhile, WB took their entire 2021
movie slate and dropped it onto HBO Max

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:28.950
without consulting any of their partners.

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:31.133
That move specifically appalled Nolan,

00:09:31.133 --> 00:09:35.216
who felt blindsided by that decision,
and he ditched Warner as a partner.

00:09:35.216 --> 00:09:37.466
Likewise, the government's decision
to use the bombs instead

00:09:37.466 --> 00:09:39.200
of sharing the knowledge
to the wider world

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:43.016
led to a rift between Oppenheimer
and the military as his advice was ignored

00:09:43.016 --> 00:09:46.433
and he was pushed further and further away
from the decision making process.

00:09:46.433 --> 00:09:48.866
Years and years later,
after the dust settles, both men

00:09:48.866 --> 00:09:50.266
returned to what they were doing before.

00:09:50.266 --> 00:09:53.366
Oppenheimer teaches, and Nolan returns
to moviemaking outside

00:09:53.366 --> 00:09:56.666
the franchise ecosystem with his movies
like Dunkirk and Tenet.

00:09:56.666 --> 00:10:00.233
But despite this, they both continue
to work against their former benefactors.

00:10:00.233 --> 00:10:01.550
We've already spoken about how Nolan

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:04.166
and WB had a falling out,
but Oppenheimer's insistence

00:10:04.166 --> 00:10:06.383
to speak out against the H-bomb
program earned him

00:10:06.383 --> 00:10:09.533
the ire of government figures like Lewis
Strauss, and his security

00:10:09.533 --> 00:10:12.616
clearance was revoked
in an embarrassing semipublic trial.

00:10:12.616 --> 00:10:15.600
And you see, this is where we're at in
Nolan story currently.

00:10:15.616 --> 00:10:18.850
Despite the bad blood our Strauss
stand in Warner Brothers is trying to woo

00:10:18.866 --> 00:10:20.633
him back to work on their projects.

00:10:20.633 --> 00:10:23.100
But our allegorical Oppenheimer,
Christopher Nolan,

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:25.433
isn't really interested in doing things
the way they want to.

00:10:25.466 --> 00:10:28.483
Nolan just flat out is not interested
in working on the studio

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:32.183
superhero mega franchises anymore,
and he's keen to let the world know it.

00:10:32.183 --> 00:10:34.883
During the press tour for Oppenheimer,
when asked if he would do any more

00:10:34.883 --> 00:10:38.033
superhero films,
Nolan replied with a very quick and curt

00:10:38.066 --> 00:10:43.066
“No.” And it's obvious to see why.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:43.316
I mean,

00:10:43.316 --> 00:10:46.666
just look at what these mega franchises
have done to cinema in the past few years.

00:10:46.666 --> 00:10:48.533
We've talked about it
a lot on this channel already,

00:10:48.533 --> 00:10:49.816
but studios are pumping more

00:10:49.816 --> 00:10:53.416
and more money into their big brand
driven, superpowered action movies.

00:10:53.416 --> 00:10:56.416
Chasing the box office numbers
pulled in by those Dark Knight films.

00:10:56.416 --> 00:10:58.316
But that just isn't a sustainable model.

00:10:58.316 --> 00:11:02.116
In a lot of ways those early Chris Nolan
Batman movies set off a chain reaction

00:11:02.116 --> 00:11:05.933
that ultimately led to massive bombs
like The Flash earlier this year.

00:11:05.933 --> 00:11:06.316
To me,

00:11:06.316 --> 00:11:09.466
this is actually the most compelling
comparison between these two men.

00:11:09.466 --> 00:11:11.716
For Oppy there was this worry
that the atomic test at Los

00:11:11.716 --> 00:11:13.550
Alamos was going to set off
a chain reaction

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:15.950
that would ignite the atmosphere
and destroy the world.

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:17.850
Now, obviously
that didn't end up happening.

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:19.550
But in the most chilling scene
in the whole movie,

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:23.000
Oppenheimer tells us that he's afraid
that he did set off a chain reaction

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:24.083
that would destroy the world.

00:11:24.083 --> 00:11:25.166
It wasn't one bomb.

00:11:25.166 --> 00:11:28.633
It was unlocking the power of the atom
in general, leading to bigger

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:31.850
and bigger weaponry that would
ultimately lead to nuclear annihilation.

00:11:31.883 --> 00:11:35.900
Nolan, meanwhile, inadvertently set off
a similar chain reaction across Hollywood.

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:38.300
With all the money
now getting funneled into mega franchises,

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:40.050
he effectively helped to kill off the low

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:42.233
and mid-budget movies
that started his career.

00:11:42.233 --> 00:11:43.916
And now, as those big movies

00:11:43.916 --> 00:11:47.266
fail one after the other,
the industry as a whole is collapsing.

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:50.950
The world of Hollywood
has literally been set on fire.

00:11:50.966 --> 00:11:54.600
That being said, this is where our big
twist of this whole allegory comes in.

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:57.983
Because Oppenheimer, the film has
so many parallels to Nolan's own life.

00:11:57.983 --> 00:12:01.766
We can actually use the end of Oppy’s
story to predict what Nolan is going to do

00:12:01.766 --> 00:12:02.183
next.

00:12:02.183 --> 00:12:04.516
Clearly,
Nolan isn't done with big budget cinema

00:12:04.516 --> 00:12:05.900
and he's doing his best to use his name

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:07.966
to pull weight
within the world of filmmaking.

00:12:07.966 --> 00:12:10.250
Nolan will want to pull things back
to a reasonable scale,

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:13.516
telling stories that are good
while also pushing the artform forward.

00:12:13.516 --> 00:12:15.766
And to do it, just like Oppenheimer
tried to join

00:12:15.766 --> 00:12:19.050
the development of the hydrogen bomb
to steer it in a direction he preferred,

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:22.316
I suspect Nolan will be going back
to the mega franchises.

00:12:22.316 --> 00:12:25.350
In fact, I think we can even pinpoint
which ones he's going to do.

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:26.816
Hear me out and quote me later.

00:12:26.816 --> 00:12:30.500
Christopher Nolan will direct a Star Wars
movie, also a James Bond movie.

00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:31.733
Why those two specifically?

00:12:31.733 --> 00:12:32.633
Well, first of all, a James

00:12:32.633 --> 00:12:35.616
Bond project would fit Nolan's down
to Earth style perfectly.

00:12:35.633 --> 00:12:37.050
He's even expressed interest in it.

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:39.116
During the press tour
for Oppenheimer he said this:

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:48.383
But the more interesting direction

00:12:48.383 --> 00:12:51.600
here, the possibility of a Christopher
Nolan's Star Wars film.

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:52.133
You see,

00:12:52.133 --> 00:12:56.333
like so many of us here on the Internet,
Nolan has some strong capital ‘O’ opinions

00:12:56.333 --> 00:13:00.233
about the Star Wars franchise,
especially what makes them so successful.

00:13:00.233 --> 00:13:02.600
The way he sees it,
a lot of people think that the Star Wars

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:03.650
franchise has only worked

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:06.833
because of its story,
and he disagrees with that entirely.

00:13:06.833 --> 00:13:08.816
He thinks that
while the plot is important,

00:13:08.816 --> 00:13:11.633
these sorts of movies
also need to push technology along.

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:14.333
To make something new and cool
with its visual effects.

00:13:14.333 --> 00:13:17.066
It has to be spectacle alongside story.

00:13:17.066 --> 00:13:19.666
And by the looks of it, he's
not just interested in diagnosing

00:13:19.666 --> 00:13:23.566
the problem, it seems like he's interested
in finding the cure himself.

00:13:23.566 --> 00:13:24.766
During that same interview

00:13:24.766 --> 00:13:27.766
where he said point blank that he wouldn't
do any more superhero movies,

00:13:27.766 --> 00:13:32.033
Nolan specifically declined to answer
when he was asked about Star Wars.

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:38.016
Sounds to me like
someone who's in negotiations with Disney.

00:13:38.016 --> 00:13:40.466
Which makes me wonder,
will Nolan be able to make it happen?

00:13:40.466 --> 00:13:42.200
If he is able to make more movies

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:45.766
in these mega franchises, will he be able
to steer them into something better?

00:13:45.766 --> 00:13:48.416
Something more meaningful?
Something more cinematic?

00:13:48.416 --> 00:13:52.216
Or will his guidance be ignored
just like Oppenheimer's was?

00:13:52.216 --> 00:13:55.216
But hey! That's just a theory.

00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:58.466
A FILM THEORY! aaaaaaaand cut.

00:13:58.466 --> 00:14:00.266
And hey, If you want to check out

00:14:00.266 --> 00:14:03.266
some of our older film theories
about the Chris Nolan Batman movies

00:14:03.266 --> 00:14:05.966
that started this whole chain reaction,
why not check out our video

00:14:05.966 --> 00:14:08.716
about how the Joker
is secretly a billionaire?

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:11.716
Or if you want a really old school
classic film theory, check out the video

00:14:11.716 --> 00:14:17.216
about how Batman actually destroyed
